QB.add(26,[{"id":26521,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Structure <b>B</b> is a right-handed coil. Which bonds stabilise it?","o":["Hydrogen bonds between a carbonyl group and the amide hydrogen 4 residues later","Disulphide bonds between cysteine side chains","Hydrogen bonds between side-by-side strands with R groups above and below","Hydrophobic side chains buried in the core","Covalent peptide bonds between adjacent R groups"],"w":["Disulphides are extra covalent links, not what forms a helix.","That describes the β-sheet (C).","Burying nonpolar groups drives folding, not this coil.","Peptide bonds join backbone, not R groups."],"e":"B is an α-helix: the backbone winds into a right-handed helix stabilised by hydrogen bonds from the carbonyl of one amino acid to the amide hydrogen 4 residues later. It forms readily because it makes optimal use of internal hydrogen bonds.","k":"α-helix: C=O to N–H, i+4.","img":{"src":"26/o_protein","cap":"Levels of protein structure. Image: LadyofHats, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Which groups form the α-helix H bond?","Which groups form the α-helix H bond?","Carboxyl of one residue; Amide H 4 residues later"]]},{"id":26522,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Name the level of structure labelled <b>E</b>, where several polypeptide chains associate, as in haemoglobin.","a":["quaternary","quaternary structure"],"e":"E is quaternary structure: the arrangement of multiple polypeptide chains, e.g. haemoglobin and hexokinase.","k":"E = quaternary.","img":{"src":"26/o_protein","cap":"Levels of protein structure. Image: LadyofHats, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Quaternary structure","Quaternary structure","Multiple polypeptide chains; Arranged in complexes"],["","What are 2 well known examples of quaternary structure?","What are 2 well known examples of quaternary structure?","Haemoglobin; Hexokinase"],["","Image card: Which enzyme is this quaternary structure? How many chains?","Which enzyme is this quaternary structure? How many chains?","Hexokinase; Two"]]},{"id":26523,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"In sickle cell anaemia one change, glutamate to valine, occurs in haemoglobin. At which level does it act first, and why can it matter?","o":["Primary structure (A); it can alter the tertiary structure and function","Secondary structure (B); it breaks every hydrogen bond","Quaternary structure (E); it removes one whole chain","Tertiary structure (D) only; the sequence is unchanged","Secondary structure (C); it turns a β-sheet into an α-helix"],"w":["One substitution does not break every bond.","The chains are still present.","The sequence is exactly what changes.","The change is in sequence, not a direct switch of fold."],"e":"A substitution changes the primary sequence (A). The primary sequence determines the final tertiary structure, so a single change can alter the fold or a small critical area and affect function and interactions.","k":"Sequence → shape → function.","img":{"src":"26/o_protein","cap":"Levels of protein structure. Image: LadyofHats, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","What determines the final tertiary structure?","What determines the final tertiary structure?","The primary sequence"],["","What 2 things can an amino acid change do?","What 2 things can an amino acid change do?","Alter tertiary structure; Change a small critical area"],["","What are the 4 levels of protein structure?","What are the 4 levels of protein structure?","Primary; Secondary; Tertiary; Quaternary; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":26524,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Name the extended zig-zag structure labelled <b>C</b>.","a":["beta sheet","β-sheet","beta-sheet","β sheet","beta pleated sheet","b sheet","anti-parallel beta sheet","antiparallel beta sheet"],"e":"C is a β-sheet (here anti-parallel): hydrogen bonds between adjacent chains, R groups above and below; parallel and anti-parallel sheets have different bonding patterns.","k":"C = β-sheet.","img":{"src":"26/o_protein","cap":"Levels of protein structure. Image: LadyofHats, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Beta-barrel","Beta-barrel","Anti-parallel β-sheet; Forming a barrel"],["","Are hydrogen bonding patterns the same in parallel and anti-parallel sheets?","Are hydrogen bonding patterns the same in parallel and anti-parallel sheets?","No, different"]]},{"id":26525,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Which label shows the overall three-dimensional shape of a single folded polypeptide, as in myoglobin?","o":["D","A","B","C","E"],"w":["A is the primary sequence.","B is an α-helix (secondary).","C is a β-sheet (secondary).","E has several chains (quaternary)."],"e":"D shows tertiary structure: the 3-D fold of one polypeptide (e.g. myoglobin, TNF-α, β-barrels).","k":"D = tertiary.","img":{"src":"26/o_protein","cap":"Levels of protein structure. Image: LadyofHats, Public domain, Wikimedia Commons; labels removed."},"rn":[["","Image card: Which tertiary structure example is this?","Which tertiary structure example is this?","Myoglobin"]]},{"id":26001,"t":"Amino Acids","f":"ps03","s":"Which three groups do all amino acids share, and which gives each its identity?","o":["Amino, carboxyl and R group; the R group gives identity","Amino, carboxyl and R group; the amino group gives identity","Amino, phosphate and R group; the R group gives identity","Amino, carboxyl and a sugar; the sugar gives identity","Carboxyl and R group only; the carboxyl gives identity"],"e":"All amino acids have an <b>amino (-NH₂)</b> terminus, a <b>carboxyl (-COOH)</b> terminus and an <b>R group</b> (side chain). The <b>R group defines the amino acid</b> and its chemical identity. Biology uses <b>20</b> common amino acids. “Acid” = the carboxyl H can dissociate.","w":["The R group (side chain) gives identity.","No phosphate.","No sugar.","Amino group too."],"k":"Amino, carboxyl, R group.","rn":[["","What gives an amino acid its identity?","What gives an amino acid its identity?","The R group"]]},{"id":26002,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"Which pairing of R-group class and chemistry is correct?","o":["Positively charged – hydrophilic, charged at pH 7","Non-polar aliphatic – hydrophilic, charged at pH 7","Polar uncharged – hydrophobic, charged at pH 7","Negatively charged – hydrophilic, uncharged at pH 7","Aromatic – hydrophilic and always charged"],"e":"Classes: <b>non-polar aliphatic</b> – hydrophobic; <b>aromatic</b> – hydrophobic but functional groups may alter polarity; <b>polar</b> – uncharged, hydrophilic; <b>positive (basic)</b> and <b>negative (acidic)</b> – hydrophilic with <b>significant charge at pH 7</b>. Learn types/chemistry; examples not required.","w":["Aliphatic is hydrophobic.","Polar uncharged is hydrophilic.","Charged at pH 7.","Aromatic is largely hydrophobic."],"k":"R-group classes.","rn":[["","Positively charged R groups: chemistry?","Positively charged R groups: chemistry?","Hydrophilic; Significant charge at pH 7"]]},{"id":26003,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"Why is it significant that charged R groups carry their charge at pH 7?","o":["pH 7 is about cellular pH, so the charges shape proteins","pH 7 is lab pH only; in cells the charges are lost","Charge appears only after the protein is denatured","At pH 7 the charges make the R groups hydrophobic","Proteins are never found at pH 7"],"e":"The important point is that the charge exists at <b>pH 7</b>, about the <b>physiological pH</b>; charges become important for structure.","w":["pH 7 ≈ physiological.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Charge at physiological pH.","rn":[["","Why do the R group charges matter?","Why do the R group charges matter?","Become important in protein structure"]]},{"id":26004,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"Why can tyrosine, though largely hydrophobic, form hydrogen bonds with water?","o":["Its aromatic ring carries a hydroxyl group","Its aromatic ring carries a positive charge at pH 7","Its side chain contains sulphur","Its side chain is just a hydrogen","It has no ring, only a polar chain"],"e":"Aromatic amino acids are hydrophobic (C and H) but <b>functional groups</b> such as a <b>hydroxyl</b> give some polarity – tyrosine's OH lets it make hydrogen bonds.","w":["It is uncharged.","That is cysteine.","That is glycine.","It is aromatic."],"k":"Tyrosine OH → H-bonds.","rn":[["","Why can aromatic amino acids interact with water to a degree?","Why can aromatic amino acids interact with water to a degree?","Functional groups added on; e.g. hydroxyl group"]]},{"id":26005,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Name the amino acid whose sulphur-containing side chain can form covalent bonds with another of its kind.","e":"Two cysteine SH groups within ~4 Å are oxidised to a disulphide bond (broken by reduction).","k":"Cysteine.","a":["cysteine","cys","c"],"rn":[["","Which amino acid forms disulphide bonds?","Which amino acid forms disulphide bonds?","Cysteine"],["","Why is cysteine special?","Why is cysteine special?","Sulphur in R group; Forms disulphide bonds"]]},{"id":26007,"t":"Levels of Protein Structure","s":"Which correctly describes the four levels of protein structure?","o":["1° sequence; 2° local folding; 3° one chain’s shape; 4° several chains","1° sequence; 2° one chain’s shape; 3° local folding; 4° several chains","1° local folding; 2° sequence; 3° one chain’s shape; 4° several chains","1° sequence; 2° local folding; 3° several chains; 4° one chain’s shape","1° shape; 2° sequence; 3° helices; 4° sheets"],"e":"Four levels (IMPORTANT): <b>primary</b> (linear sequence), <b>secondary</b> (local folding – β-sheets, α-helices, turns), <b>tertiary</b> (3-D shape of <b>one</b> chain), <b>quaternary</b> (how <b>different chains</b> are arranged).","w":["2° and 3° swapped.","1° and 2° swapped.","3° and 4° swapped.","Not."],"k":"Four levels.","rn":[["","Tertiary structure in one phrase?","Tertiary structure in one phrase?","Folding relative to other components; 3D shape of one chain"],["","What do these interactions do to the chain?","What do these interactions do to the chain?","Stabilise it; Hold its folded shape"],["","Secondary structure","Secondary structure","Local folding; Into unique conformations"]]},{"id":26008,"t":"Amino Acids","f":"ps11","s":"What is the polarity of a polypeptide chain?","o":["N (amino) terminus → C (carboxyl) terminus","C (carboxyl) terminus → N (amino) terminus","5′ end → 3′ end","R group → backbone","It has no direction"],"e":"Primary structure: linear sequence, e.g. N–DFGGEDRFAPWCGHCKLRF–C. Polarity: <b>N → C</b> (amino to carboxyl). Shorthand letters (G = glycine, F = phenylalanine) need not be memorised.","w":["Reverse.","Nucleic acids.","Not.","It has polarity."],"k":"Chains run N → C.","rn":[["","What 3 parts do all amino acids have?","What 3 parts do all amino acids have?","Amino (-NH2) terminus; Carboxyl (-COOH) terminus; R group"],["","C (of a polypeptide)","C (of a polypeptide)","Carboxyl (acid) terminus"],["","N (of a polypeptide)","N (of a polypeptide)","Amino terminus"]]},{"id":26009,"t":"Primary Structure","f":"ps12","s":"How is a peptide bond formed?","o":["Water is removed between carboxyl C and amino N","Water is added between carboxyl C and amino N","Water is removed between two R groups","Two sulphurs are oxidised","A phosphate is added by a kinase"],"e":"The <b>ribosome</b> generates the peptide bond: <b>water is removed</b> and a bond forms between the <b>carbon</b> (carboxyl) and <b>nitrogen</b> (amino) – e.g. glycylalanine.","w":["Removed.","Backbone groups.","Disulphide.","Not."],"k":"Peptide bond: condensation.","rn":[["","What is removed when a peptide bond forms?","What is removed when a peptide bond forms?","Water"]]},{"id":26010,"t":"Primary Structure","s":"The C–N peptide bond length is 0.132 nm, compared with 0.147 nm (single) and 0.127 nm (double). What does this indicate?","o":["Partial double bond character – rigid and planar","Pure single bond character – free rotation","Pure double bond character – rotation only when heated","Partial double bond character – free rotation around it","An ionic bond"],"e":"Peptide bonds are <b>not single</b>, <b>not double</b>; their length is in between – <b>partial double bond character</b>, “<b>rigid and planar</b>”, no rotation around it (rotation on flanking bonds: phi and psi).","w":["It is between single and double.","Partial.","No rotation.","Covalent."],"k":"Partial double → rigid, planar.","rn":[["","What character does the peptide bond have?","What character does the peptide bond have?","Partial double bond character"]]},{"id":26011,"t":"Chaperones","s":"Why does the rigid peptide bond matter for folding?","o":["It cuts possible folds from ~10⁴⁰ to ~10¹²","It cuts possible folds from ~10¹² to ~10⁴","It raises possible folds from ~10¹² to ~10⁴⁰","It allows free rotation, so any fold is reachable","It prevents folding entirely"],"e":"With full rotation, possible folds would be “like 10 to the power of 40”; rigid peptide bonds reduce this to “about 10 to the 12” – reducing the folding problem.","w":["Numbers wrong.","Reduces.","Rigid.","Not."],"k":"Rigid bond reduces folds.","rn":[["","Possible fold combinations with rigid peptide bonds?","Possible fold combinations with rigid peptide bonds?","About 10^12"],["","Possible fold combinations with full rotation?","Possible fold combinations with full rotation?","About 10^40"]]},{"id":26012,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"The negative carboxyl of glutamic acid attracts the positive amino group of lysine. Name this interaction.","e":"A salt bridge is an electrostatic interaction; it stabilises most in the hydrophobic interior.","k":"Salt bridge.","a":["salt bridge","electrostatic","ionic bond","electrostatic interaction","ionic interaction"],"rn":[["","Salt bridge","Salt bridge","Ionic (electrostatic) interaction"],["","What does a salt bridge in the interior give?","What does a salt bridge in the interior give?","Significant stabilisation of protein structure"],["","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","Inside the hydrophobic interior"]]},{"id":26013,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which statement about hydrogen bonds in proteins is correct?","o":["One is weak, but many together give rigidity","One is stronger than a disulphide bond","They are covalent, so they rarely break","They occur only in DNA base pairs","Many together are weak, as they cancel out"],"e":"<b>Hydrogen bond</b>: an O and an H on an electronegative N. <b>One is weak</b> (“rubbish”, making and breaking), but <b>many together are strong</b>.","w":["Weaker.","Non-covalent.","Proteins too.","Many are strong."],"k":"Many H-bonds = strong.","rn":[["","What do many hydrogen bonds together give?","What do many hydrogen bonds together give?","Structural rigidity; Quite strong"]]},{"id":26014,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"How do van der Waals interactions arise between uncharged methyl groups?","o":["Temporary dipoles from uneven electron clouds","Permanent opposite charges on the groups","Shared electrons forming covalent bonds","Hydrogen bonds between the methyl hydrogens","Peptide bonds"],"e":"<b>Van der Waals</b>: electron clouds are never perfect – brief partial positive/negative patches (<b>dipoles</b>) allow attraction (e.g. CH₃ of Val, Ala, Val). Also an entropic component.","w":["Uncharged groups.","Non-covalent.","Not.","Not."],"k":"VdW = temporary dipoles.","rn":[["","What is Van der Waals Interaction?","What is Van der Waals Interaction?","Attraction from uneven electron clouds; Between uncharged hydrophobic groups"]]},{"id":26015,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","f":"ps14","s":"How is a disulphide bond formed and broken?","o":["Two cysteine SH within ~4 Å are oxidised; reduction breaks it","Two cysteine SH within ~4 Å are reduced; oxidation breaks it","Two methionine S within ~4 Å are oxidised; reduction breaks it","Two cysteine SH within ~40 Å are oxidised; hydrolysis breaks it","Hydrogen bonds between sulphurs"],"e":"Cysteine has S and H; within about <b>4 Å</b>, <b>oxidation</b> removes the hydrogens and the sulphurs bond <b>covalently</b>. <b>Reduction</b> (or significant heat) breaks it. Interchain or intrachain.","w":["Swapped.","Cysteine.","~4 Å; reduction.","Covalent."],"k":"Disulphide: oxidation forms, reduction breaks.","rn":[["","What reaction breaks a disulphide bond?","What reaction breaks a disulphide bond?","Reduction"]]},{"id":26016,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which statement about disulphide bonds is correct?","o":["Covalent, very strong and limit misfolding","Covalent, very weak and increase misfolding","Non-covalent, very strong and limit misfolding","The weakest interaction in proteins","Form between any two amino acids"],"e":"Disulphide bonds: actual <b>covalent</b> interaction, <b>incredibly strong</b> (“like a metal bracket”), quite thermally stable; <b>reduce the number of ways a protein can MISFOLD</b>.","w":["Strong; reduce misfolding.","Covalent.","Strongest.","Cysteines."],"k":"Disulphides strong, limit misfolding."},{"id":26017,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which ranking of individual interaction strength is correct?","o":["Disulphide > electrostatic > H-bond > van der Waals","Electrostatic > disulphide > H-bond > van der Waals","Disulphide > H-bond > van der Waals > electrostatic","Van der Waals > H-bond > electrostatic > disulphide","All roughly equal"],"e":"Disulphide: way stronger than electrostatic, stronger than H-bond, a lot stronger than van der Waals.","w":["Disulphide strongest.","Order wrong.","Reverse.","Not."],"k":"Interaction strengths.","rn":[["","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","Electrostatic attractions; Hydrogen bonds; Van der Waals attractions"],["","What holds chains together at the interface?","What holds chains together at the interface?","Mostly non-covalent; H bonds, van der Waals; Electrostatic"],["","Weakest 2 protein interactions?","Weakest 2 protein interactions?","3. Hydrogen bond; 4. Van der Waals"]]},{"id":26018,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Which are the types of secondary structure reviewed?","o":["β-sheets, α-helices and β-turns","β-sheets, α-helices and domains","Primary sequences and tetramers","Disulphide bridges and salt bridges","β-sheets and α-helices only"],"e":"Secondary structure: proteins <b>locally folded</b> into strands, turns and helices: β-sheet, α-helix, β-turn.","w":["Domains are tertiary.","Not.","Interactions.","Turns too."],"k":"Secondary types.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of secondary structure?","What are the 3 types of secondary structure?","β-sheet (parallel and anti-parallel); α-helix; β-turns"],["","What do turns and loops connect?","What do turns and loops connect?","Chains between secondary structures; e.g. α-helices and β-sheets"],["","What 3 secondary structures are there?","What 3 secondary structures are there?","Strands; Turns; Helices"]]},{"id":26019,"t":"Secondary Structure","f":"ps17","s":"What distinguishes anti-parallel from parallel β-sheets?","o":["Anti-parallel: strands opposite, straight H-bonds; parallel: same way, slanted","Anti-parallel: strands same way, straight H-bonds; parallel: opposite, slanted","Anti-parallel: strands opposite, slanted H-bonds; parallel: same way, straight","Anti-parallel lack hydrogen bonds","No difference"],"e":"<b>Anti-parallel</b>: strands opposite (down, up, down), H-bonds straight across. <b>Parallel</b>: all same direction, H-bonds slightly <b>skewed</b> (“squint”).","w":["Swapped directions.","Swapped H-bonds.","Both have H-bonds.","Different."],"k":"Anti-parallel vs parallel.","rn":[["","Anti-parallel β-sheet","Anti-parallel β-sheet","Adjacent strands run; Opposite directions"]]},{"id":26020,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Why must R groups be relatively small for β-sheets to form?","o":["R groups jut above and below, so bulky ones clash","R groups form the hydrogen bonds between strands","R groups must be charged for strands to pair","Large R groups strengthen the sheet","R groups are removed"],"e":"H-bonds form between <b>adjacent chains</b> with R groups protruding above and below; <b>R groups must be relatively small</b>. Tyr, Phe, Trp, Thr, Val, Ile tend to be central; proline at edges.","w":["Backbone H-bonds.","Not.","Clash.","Not."],"k":"β-sheet: small R groups.","rn":[["","Where do the R groups point in a β-sheet?","Where do the R groups point in a β-sheet?","Above and below the sheet"]]},{"id":26021,"t":"Secondary Structure","f":"ps19","s":"Which describes the α-helix?","o":["Right-handed; C=O to N–H 4 residues on; 3.6 residues/turn","Left-handed; C=O to N–H 4 residues on; 3.6 residues/turn","Right-handed; C=O to N–H 2 residues on; 10 residues/turn","Right-handed; R group to R group; 3.6 residues/turn","A double helix like DNA"],"e":"<b>α-helix</b>: right-handed; stabilised by H-bonds between carboxyl of one amino acid and amide H <b>4 residues later</b>; optimal use of internal H-bonds; <b>5.4 Å per turn (3.6 residues)</b>; R groups point outwards. A spiral staircase.","w":["Right-handed.","n+4; 3.6.","Backbone.","Not."],"k":"α-helix features.","rn":[["","Image card: What secondary structure is this? Which way does it turn?","What secondary structure is this? Which way does it turn?","α-helix; Right handed"],["","α-helix: residues per turn?","α-helix: residues per turn?","3.6"]]},{"id":26022,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Why might a sequence with many consecutive tryptophans or phenylalanines fail to form an α-helix?","o":["Bulky R groups get in the way of the spiral","They are charged and repel each other","They contain sulphur and form disulphides","Their R groups are too small to stabilise it","They are prolines"],"e":"Not all polypeptides form α-helices due to <b>bulkiness</b> and <b>ionisation state</b> of R groups. Bulky Trp/Phe in a row “will get in the way”.","w":["Bulk.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Bulky R groups hinder helices.","rn":[["","Why are bulky amino acids rare in α-helices?","Why are bulky amino acids rare in α-helices?","Block the spiral"]]},{"id":26023,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"What is meant by α-helix “sidedness”?","o":["Polar on one side, hydrophobic on the other","Two helices lying side by side, held by hydrogen bonds","Left- versus right-handedness","The N and C termini on opposite sides","Disulphides on one side"],"e":"α-helices can have <b>sidedness</b>: charged/polar on one side, hydrophobic on the other.","w":["Sidedness is within one helix.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Helix sidedness.","rn":[["","Sidedness","Sidedness","Charged/polar on one side; Hydrophobic on the other; Of an α-helix"]]},{"id":26024,"t":"Secondary Structure","f":"ps20","s":"Aquaporin's structure is described as:","o":["Almost entirely α-helical","Almost entirely β-sheet","A β-barrel","Unstructured","Mainly β-sheet with one helix"],"e":"Aquaporin contains almost entirely <b>α-helical</b> secondary structure – “chock full of α-helices” interacting with each other.","w":["Not.","GFP.","Not.","TNF-α."],"k":"Aquaporin α-helical.","rn":[["","Image card: This protein (aquaporin) is almost entirely made of which secondary structure?","This protein (aquaporin) is almost entirely made of which secondary structure?","α-helices"]]},{"id":26025,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Is the secondary structure of a protein determined by its primary structure?","o":["Yes – primary structure decides it “100%”","No – it is random and settled by chaperones","Only for β-sheets; helices are random","Yes, but only for quaternary structure","No – the solvent decides"],"e":"“<b>Primary structure determines secondary structure</b>” – whether α-helix, β-sheet, loop or turn. The primary sequence determines the final tertiary structure too.","w":["Not.","All.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sequence determines structure.","rn":[["","What determines secondary structure?","What determines secondary structure?","Primary structure"],["","What determines the final tertiary structure?","What determines the final tertiary structure?","The primary sequence"]]},{"id":26026,"t":"Secondary Structure","f":"ps21","s":"Which statement about β-turns is correct?","o":["180° turn, ~4 residues (1→4 H-bond), often proline","90° turn, ~4 residues (1→4 H-bond), often cysteine","180° turn, ~20 residues, covalently bonded","A type of α-helix","Rare (under 1% of residues)"],"e":"Nearly <b>one third</b> of amino acids are in turns/loops, connecting secondary structures. Turns bring a <b>180° change</b>; usually <b>four amino acids</b>; <b>proline</b> induces the turn/kink.","w":["180°; proline.","Four.","Not.","A third are turns/loops."],"k":"β-turn features.","rn":[["","β-turn","β-turn","A turn giving; 180° change in direction"],["","Why is proline special?","Why is proline special?","Induces a turn/kink; In the chain"],["","Why does proline help start a turn?","Why does proline help start a turn?","Its structure induces a kink"]]},{"id":26028,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"Which two amino acids have special structural roles, and why?","o":["Cysteine (disulphides) and proline (turns)","Cysteine (turns) and proline (disulphides)","Glycine and alanine (hydrophobic)","Lysine and arginine (positive)","Tryptophan and tyrosine (aromatic)"],"e":"Special amino acids to note: <b>cysteine</b> (disulphide bonds) and <b>proline</b> (turns).","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cys and Pro special.","rn":[["","What are the 4 special case amino acids?","What are the 4 special case amino acids?","Cysteine; Selenocysteine; Glycine; Proline"],["","Why is proline special?","Why is proline special?","Induces a turn/kink; In the chain"],["","Which amino acid helps cause β-turns?","Which amino acid helps cause β-turns?","Proline"]]},{"id":26029,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","f":"ps24","s":"Which structural description matches tumour necrosis factor alpha?","o":["Mainly β-sheets (with a tiny α-helix)","Mostly α-helices, like myoglobin","A β-barrel holding a fluorophore","Four haem-containing chains","Unfolded"],"e":"Tertiary examples: <b>myoglobin</b> – mostly α-helices; <b>TNF-α</b> – mainly <b>β-sheets</b>; <b>β-barrel</b> (green fluorescent protein from jellyfish) – anti-parallel strands rolled into a barrel holding the fluorophore.","w":["Myoglobin.","GFP.","Haemoglobin.","Not."],"k":"TNF-α β-sheet rich.","rn":[["","Image card: Which tertiary structure example is this? What is it mainly made of?","Which tertiary structure example is this? What is it mainly made of?","Tumour necrosis factor alpha; β-sheets"]]},{"id":26030,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Tertiary structure applies to:","o":["One peptide chain’s 3-D structure","Several chains together","The amino acid sequence","Local helices and sheets only","DNA folding"],"e":"Tertiary structure applies to <b>ONE</b> peptide chain.","w":["Quaternary.","Primary.","Secondary.","Not."],"k":"Tertiary = one chain.","rn":[["","Tertiary structure applies to how many chains?","Tertiary structure applies to how many chains?","ONE peptide chain"]]},{"id":26031,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Which statement about protein dynamics is correct?","o":["They keep their shape but move, like jelly","They are completely static, like crystals in a fixed lattice","They have no fixed structure at all","They only move when denatured","They change shape completely every second"],"e":"Pictures are static, but proteins “really aren't that static” – like a <b>jelly mould</b>. Loops “just waft around”.","w":["Proteins “really aren’t that static”.","They do.","Not.","Overall shape kept."],"k":"Proteins are dynamic.","rn":[["","Are proteins static?","Are proteins static?","No; Keep overall shape but move"]]},{"id":26032,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","f":"ps25","s":"Which statement about haemoglobin's quaternary structure is correct?","o":["Four chains with haem; interfaces mainly non-covalent","Four chains with haem; interfaces mainly disulphides","Two chains with haem; interfaces mainly non-covalent","One chain with four haem groups","Fifty chains"],"e":"<b>Haemoglobin</b>: four chains (lecture called them identical – a homotetramer), haem groups carry oxygen; <b>protein-protein interfaces</b> rich in non-covalent interactions; interchain disulphides rare. Quaternary = arrangement of chains in 3-D.","w":["Disulphides rare.","Four.","Four chains.","Not."],"k":"Haemoglobin quaternary.","rn":[["","What mainly causes non-covalent interactions?","What mainly causes non-covalent interactions?","Charge"],["","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","Electrostatic attractions; Hydrogen bonds; Van der Waals attractions"]]},{"id":26034,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Must quaternary structures always have four chains?","o":["No – it could be 2, 5 or 50 chains","Yes – always four chains","No – always exactly two chains","Only if the chains are identical","Yes – four or more"],"e":"Not always four: “could be 5, could be 50” – still quaternary; strictly a <b>protein complex</b>.","w":["Any number.","Not.","Not.","Two counts."],"k":"Quaternary: any number of chains.","rn":[["","How many chains can a quaternary structure have?","How many chains can a quaternary structure have?","Any number; e.g. 4, 5 or 50"]]},{"id":26035,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","f":"ps29","s":"In the folding funnel, what does the width represent and why does it narrow?","o":["Entropy; each structure formed limits further options","Temperature; the protein cools as it folds","Entropy; each structure formed adds more options","Molecular weight; water is lost as it folds","Number of chaperones"],"e":"Proteins fold to the <b>lowest free energy ΔG</b>. Width = <b>entropy</b> (possible shapes). After the first α-helix forms, folding options shrink – “tighter and tighter” – via molten globule and intermediates to the single <b>native structure</b>.","w":["Width = entropy.","Narrows.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Funnel width = entropy.","rn":[["","Why does forming an α-helix lower entropy?","Why does forming an α-helix lower entropy?","Limits how the rest; Can fold"]]},{"id":26036,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"What does ΔG represent in protein folding?","o":["Gibbs free energy; folding runs downhill","Gibbs free energy; folding runs uphill and needs ATP to proceed","Temperature change; folding releases heat","Mass change; water is lost on folding","Charge change"],"e":"<b>ΔG</b>: Gibbs free energy. Lower energy is like going down a hill – spontaneous. Unfolded spaghetti (higher energy) → folded (lower).","w":["Folding is downhill and more or less spontaneous.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Folding goes downhill in ΔG.","rn":[["","ΔG","ΔG","Gibbs free energy"],["","Into what structure do proteins fold?","Into what structure do proteins fold?","Lowest free energy ΔG"]]},{"id":26037,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"How stable are native proteins compared with covalent bonds?","o":["Marginally: 20–65 vs 200–460 kJ/mol for a covalent bond","Far more: 200–460 vs 20–65 kJ/mol for a covalent bond","Equally: about 200 kJ/mol each","Marginally: 2–6 vs 20–46 kJ/mol for a covalent bond","Infinitely stable"],"e":"ΔG folded–unfolded <b>only 20–65 kJ/mol</b>, vs <b>200–460 kJ/mol</b> for a covalent bond – “pretty fragile”. Numerous weak interactions maintain the fold; <b>hydrophobic interactions predominate</b>. (Numbers needn't be memorised.)","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Numbers wrong.","Not."],"k":"Marginal stability.","rn":[["","Energy to break a single covalent bond?","Energy to break a single covalent bond?","200 to 460 kJ/mol"]]},{"id":26038,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Name the main driving force of protein folding: burying non-polar side chains frees ordered water.","e":"The hydrophobic effect: clustering hydrophobic residues releases ordered water, raising its entropy.","k":"Hydrophobic effect.","a":["hydrophobic effect","hydrophobic interactions","hydrophobic interaction","hydrophobic collapse","entropy of water"],"rn":[["","What 2 things push the hydrophobic residues together?","What 2 things push the hydrophobic residues together?","Water entropy effect; Van der Waals forces"],["","Non-polar clustering: effect on entropy?","Non-polar clustering: effect on entropy?","Favourable increase in entropy"],["","Ordered water arrangement: effect on entropy?","Ordered water arrangement: effect on entropy?","Decrease in entropy; Unfavourable"]]},{"id":26039,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"What mainly drives protein folding?","o":["Hydrophobic interactions drive folding","Disulphide bonds drive protein folding, locking each step","Hydrogen bonds drive protein folding","Chaperones drive all protein folding","Electrostatics drive protein folding"],"e":"“<b>Hydrophobic interactions driving protein folding. That's the key.</b> If you can't remember anything else, that's the bit I want you to remember.”","w":["Hydrophobic interactions are the key.","Not.","Some.","Not."],"k":"Hydrophobic interactions drive folding.","rn":[["","What DRIVES folding and stabilises it?","What DRIVES folding and stabilises it?","Hydrophobic interactions"],["","Why is folding spontaneous?","Why is folding spontaneous?","Huge drive to bury; The hydrophobic core"]]},{"id":26040,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Name the partly folded, compact state formed by fast hydrophobic collapse before slow rearrangement.","e":"Unfolded → fast collapse (molten globule) → slow rearrangement to the native state.","k":"Molten globule.","a":["molten globule","molten-globule"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of folding via the molten globule?","What are the 3 steps of folding via the molten globule?","Unfolded protein; Hydrophobic collapse; Internal core rearrangement"],["","Molten Globule","Molten Globule","Collapsed intermediate after hydrophobic collapse"]]},{"id":26041,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"During hydrophobic collapse, what happens to the entropy of water and protein?","o":["Water entropy rises; protein entropy falls","Water entropy falls; protein entropy rises","Both rise","Both fall","No change"],"e":"The energy plummets, water entropy becomes favourable, but the <b>entropy of the protein decreases</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Water ↑, protein ↓ entropy.","rn":[["","Image card: What is water forming around this hydrophobic molecule? What happens to entropy?","What is water forming around this hydrophobic molecule? What happens to entropy?","Ordered solvent shell; Entropy decreases (unfavourable)"],["","When the core forms, what happens to the entropy of the protein itself?","When the core forms, what happens to the entropy of the protein itself?","Decreases"],["","Ordered water arrangement: effect on entropy?","Ordered water arrangement: effect on entropy?","Decrease in entropy; Unfavourable"]]},{"id":26042,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Why doesn't water simply unfold a folded protein?","o":["The hidden hydrophobic core keeps it stable","Water cannot reach the surface of proteins inside cells","Proteins are sealed by covalent bonds","Water is hydrophobic","Proteins have no hydrophobic residues"],"e":"The hydrophobic core is always hidden; the drive to remove the oily core from water makes folding spontaneous. (Also van der Waals: thousands make a strong core.)","w":["The drive to hide the oily core keeps the fold.","Mostly non-covalent.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Hidden core stabilises fold.","rn":[["","Where is the hydrophobic core always found?","Where is the hydrophobic core always found?","Hidden in the centre"]]},{"id":26043,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Why do hydrogen bonds form “cooperatively” during folding?","o":["One makes the next more likely","They form all at once at random","Each one makes the next less likely","They need chaperones to form","They are covalent"],"e":"Hydrogen bonds form <b>cooperatively</b>: one makes the next more likely.","w":["Not.","Reverse.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cooperative H-bonds.","rn":[["","Cooperative hydrogen bonding","Cooperative hydrogen bonding","One forming makes; The next more likely"]]},{"id":26044,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Where does a salt bridge give the most significant stabilisation?","o":["In the hydrophobic interior","On the surface in water","In the cytoplasm away from protein","Only in DNA","At disulphide bonds"],"e":"Salt bridge strength increases if formed within the <b>hydrophobic interior</b> – significant stabilisation.","w":["Weaker.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Buried salt bridges stronger.","rn":[["","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","Inside the hydrophobic interior"],["","What does a salt bridge in the interior give?","What does a salt bridge in the interior give?","Significant stabilisation of protein structure"]]},{"id":26045,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"How is entropy more accurately defined?","o":["Multiplicity – number of possible states","Disorder – how messy a molecule looks under a microscope","Temperature","Heat only","Order"],"e":"Entropy as disorder is basic; more accurately <b>multiplicity</b>: number of possible states. (Not required, but accurate.)","w":["Disorder is the basic version; multiplicity is more accurate.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Entropy = multiplicity.","rn":[["","Multiplicity","Multiplicity","Variety of possible states; Of a molecule/system"]]},{"id":26046,"t":"Protein Domains","s":"Name a separately folded region of a protein with its own function.","e":"Domains fold separately; similar functions (e.g. DNA binding) give chemically similar domains.","k":"Domain.","a":["domain","protein domain","domains"],"rn":[["","Protein domain","Protein domain","Protein region with; A distinctive function"]]},{"id":26047,"t":"Protein Domains","s":"Why do different DNA-binding domains have chemically similar sequences?","o":["They do the same job: bind groove and backbone","They come from one gene that has been copied many times","They are random similarities","They are hydrophobic only","They contain no amino acids"],"e":"DNA-binding domains have similar (chemically) stretches “because they have to do the same thing”: bind the major groove, phosphate backbone, make H-bonds (hydrophilic residues), recognise bases.","w":["They are similar because the job is the same.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Similar function → similar motifs."},{"id":26048,"t":"Protein Domains","s":"Name an enzyme that breaks down protein.","e":"A protease breaks down protein; many proteins have protease domains.","k":"Protease.","a":["protease","proteases","peptidase","proteinase"],"rn":[["","Protease","Protease","Enzyme that breaks down protein; Molecular scissors"]]},{"id":26049,"t":"Chaperones","s":"Name the proteins (e.g. GroEL-ES) that help some proteins fold correctly.","e":"Chaperones guide folding of some proteins; GroEL-ES is a hollow cup with a lid.","k":"Chaperones.","a":["chaperones","chaperone","molecular chaperones","chaperonins","chaperonin"],"rn":[["","Chaperones","Chaperones","Proteins that guide; Others to fold correctly"]]},{"id":26050,"t":"Chaperones","s":"What is the structure of the GroEL-ES complex?","o":["A hollow cup with a lid","A single α-helix","A β-barrel with a fluorophore","A haem-containing tetramer","A membrane channel"],"e":"GroEL-ES from E. coli: hollow cup with a lid; protein goes in, lid on, folded protein out.","w":["Not.","GFP.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GroEL-ES = cup + lid."},{"id":26051,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","f":"ps36","s":"What is denaturation and can it be reversed?","o":["Loss of non-covalent bonds; some refold","Unfolding by breaking peptide bonds; no protein can ever refold","Unfolding by breaking non-covalent bonds; none can refold","Adding amino acids","Caused only by chaperones"],"e":"Solvents that <b>break non-covalent interactions</b> (and <b>reduce disulphides</b>) <b>unfold</b> proteins, e.g. <b>urea</b>. Some proteins (e.g. <b>ribonuclease</b>) <b>recover</b> when it's removed. pH changes also denature.","w":["Non-covalent; ribonuclease refolds.","Some refold.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Denaturation; ribonuclease refolds.","rn":[["","Covalent vs non-covalent","Covalent vs non-covalent","Covalent: actual physical bond; Non-covalent: no shared electrons"],["","What unfolds proteins?","What unfolds proteins?","Solvents breaking non-covalent interactions; And reducing disulphides"],["","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","Electrostatic attractions; Hydrogen bonds; Van der Waals attractions"]]},{"id":26052,"t":"Protein Denaturation","s":"Why does egg white turn white when cooked?","o":["Albumin denatures, leaving solution","Albumin forms new peptide bonds as the heat joins the chains","Water is added to the albumin","Chaperones activate","New disulphides make new proteins"],"e":"Egg white is concentrated <b>albumin</b>; denatured, protein comes out of solution and turns white – “literally why your egg turns white”. It doesn't refold (a cooked egg never goes back).","w":["It denatures and comes out of solution.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Egg white = albumin denaturation.","rn":[["","Why does egg white turn white when cooked?","Why does egg white turn white when cooked?","Protein denatures; Comes out of solution; Optical properties change"]]},{"id":26053,"t":"Protein Mutations","f":"ps37","s":"How does the sickle cell mutation alter haemoglobin and red cells?","o":["Glu → Val puts valine on the surface; Hb sticks in chains","Val → Glu puts glutamate on the surface; Hb sticks in chains","Glu → Val puts valine in the core; Hb unfolds","It removes haem from the chains","It adds a disulphide"],"e":"<b>Sickle cell anaemia</b>: inherited haemoglobin disorder, <b>GLU → VAL</b>. One change puts a <b>hydrophobic valine outside</b>, letting haemoglobins attach into chains inside the erythrocyte → <b>sickle shape</b>. Thought to protect against malaria. (Recording said “glutamine”; slide: glutamic acid.)","w":["Glu → Val.","Surface.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sickle cell: Glu → Val.","rn":[["","Image card: Left vs right: what is happening to the haemoglobin? Why?","Left vs right: what is happening to the haemoglobin? Why?","Left: normal; Right: mutant Hb chains; Hydrophobic valine (GLU to VAL)"],["","Sickle Cell Anaemia","Sickle Cell Anaemia","Inherited disorder affecting haemoglobin; GLU to VAL"]]},{"id":26054,"t":"Protein Mutations","s":"What evolutionary advantage was linked to the sickle cell trait?","o":["Less chance of getting malaria","Better oxygen delivery at altitude","Resistance to diabetes","Longer life","Faster healing"],"e":"A student answered it decreases malaria risk; the lecturer agreed it is thought to be protective (“not 100%”).","w":["The link was to malaria.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sickle trait vs malaria."},{"id":26055,"t":"Protein Mutations","s":"How can a DNA mutation cause clinical pathology via protein structure?","o":["Changed sequence → changed structure → changed function","Changed sequence → same structure → changed RNA only","It never affects proteins","It changes lipids","It only affects quaternary bonds"],"e":"Mutations → amino acid change → altered structure/critical area → altered function → pathology.","w":["Structure changes.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mutation → structure → function.","rn":[["","What chain links mutation to disease?","What chain links mutation to disease?","Mutation affects structure; Structure affects function; Loss of function: pathology"]]},{"id":26056,"t":"Signal Sequences","s":"Name the ~15-amino-acid “postcode” that sends a protein to the ER, nucleus or an organelle.","e":"Without a signal sequence, proteins stay in the cytosol (default).","k":"Signal sequence.","a":["signal sequence","signal peptide","targeting sequence","signal sequences","leader sequence"],"rn":[["","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","The cytosol"],["","What was swapped in the famous signal sequence experiment?","What was swapped in the famous signal sequence experiment?","ER signal sequence; Onto a cytosolic protein"],["","What are 3 signal sequence destinations?","What are 3 signal sequence destinations?","ER; Nuclear; Organelle"]]},{"id":26057,"t":"Signal Sequences","s":"In the switched-signal experiment, what happened when an ER signal sequence was attached to a cytosolic protein?","o":["They swapped places","Both stayed in the cytosol","Both went to the ER","Both were degraded","Nothing changed"],"e":"Swapping signal sequences made the proteins <b>switch places</b> – signal sequences are effectively postcodes.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Switched signals switch location.","rn":[["","What was swapped in the famous signal sequence experiment?","What was swapped in the famous signal sequence experiment?","ER signal sequence; Onto a cytosolic protein"]]},{"id":26058,"t":"Signal Sequences","s":"Where do proteins without any signal sequence end up?","o":["The cytosol (default)","The nucleus, which is the default destination","The ER (default)","Mitochondria","They are secreted"],"e":"Cytosolic proteins have no signal sequence – the default.","w":["Default is cytosol.","Needs signal.","Not.","Not."],"k":"No signal → cytosol.","rn":[["","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","The cytosol"],["","Result of removing the ER signal from an ER protein?","Result of removing the ER signal from an ER protein?","Stays in the cytosol"]]},{"id":26060,"t":"Primary Structure","s":"In structural biology, what is a “residue”?","o":["A synonym for an amino acid in a chain","A water molecule left after a peptide bond forms","A disulphide bond","A chaperone","A DNA base"],"e":"<b>Residue</b> is simply a synonym for amino acid.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Residue = amino acid.","rn":[["","Residue","Residue","Synonym for amino acid; Used mainly in structural biology"]]},{"id":26061,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"A mutation replaces a surface lysine with a hydrophobic leucine. What is a plausible structural consequence?","o":["Lost salt bridge; exposed hydrophobic patch","A new disulphide bond forms between the surface residues","Stronger peptide bonds along the chain","No possible effect","Conversion to DNA"],"e":"Changing a charged surface residue to hydrophobic can remove salt bridges and expose hydrophobic surface – like Glu→Val in sickle cell causing aggregation.","w":["Leucine has no sulphur.","Not.","It can.","Not."],"k":"Surface hydrophobic mutation.","rn":[["","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","Inside the hydrophobic interior"],["","Salt bridge","Salt bridge","Ionic (electrostatic) interaction"]]},{"id":26062,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"A protein unfolds in urea but refolds correctly when urea is removed. What does this prove?","o":["Sequence holds the information for the fold","Chaperones are always required for correct folding","Urea creates new proteins","Peptide bonds broke and re-formed","Folding is random"],"e":"Refolding after removing denaturant (e.g. ribonuclease) shows the primary sequence determines structure.","w":["It refolded without chaperones.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Refolding proves sequence → structure.","rn":[["","What do these interactions do to the chain?","What do these interactions do to the chain?","Stabilise it; Hold its folded shape"]]},{"id":26063,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which pairing of interaction and covalency is correct?","o":["Disulphide – covalent; H-bond – non-covalent","Disulphide – non-covalent; H-bond – covalent","Van der Waals – covalent; salt bridge – non-covalent","Salt bridge – covalent; H-bond – non-covalent","All covalent"],"e":"Covalent: peptide, disulphide. Non-covalent: electrostatic, H-bond, van der Waals.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Covalent vs non-covalent.","rn":[["","Covalent vs non-covalent","Covalent vs non-covalent","Covalent: actual physical bond; Non-covalent: no shared electrons"],["","What is a disulphide bond?","What is a disulphide bond?","Covalent bond; Between 2 cysteines; S-S"],["","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","Electrostatic attractions; Hydrogen bonds; Van der Waals attractions"]]},{"id":26064,"t":"Protein Denaturation","s":"Which type of agent breaks disulphide bonds during denaturation?","e":"Disulphides form by oxidation and break by reduction.","k":"Reducing agent.","a":["reducing agent","reducing","reductant","reducing agents"],"rn":[["","What unfolds proteins?","What unfolds proteins?","Solvents breaking non-covalent interactions; And reducing disulphides"]]},{"id":26065,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"Which amino acid would you expect in the hydrophobic core of a protein?","o":["Leucine","Lysine","Aspartic acid","Glutamic acid","Arginine"],"e":"Non-polar aliphatic (Ala, Ile, Leu, Met) are hydrophobic – buried in the core.","w":["Charged.","Charged.","Charged.","Charged."],"k":"Leucine in core.","rn":[["","Leu (L)","Leu (L)","Leucine"],["","Which 4 aliphatic amino acids are hydrophobic?","Which 4 aliphatic amino acids are hydrophobic?","Alanine; Isoleucine; Leucine; Valine"]]},{"id":26066,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which amino acid is most likely on the protein surface interacting with water?","o":["Glutamic acid","Isoleucine (hydrophobic)","Leucine","Phenylalanine","Valine"],"e":"Charged hydrophilic residues tend to face solvent.","w":["Hydrophobic residues hide in the core.","Hydrophobic.","Hydrophobic.","Hydrophobic."],"k":"Charged residues on surface.","rn":[["","Why is glutamic acid negative?","Why is glutamic acid negative?","It is an acid"],["","Glu (E)","Glu (E)","Glutamic acid"],["","Which 2 amino acids show an electrostatic attraction?","Which 2 amino acids show an electrostatic attraction?","Glutamic acid; Lysine"]]},{"id":26067,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Which explains why a hydrophobic protein that never folded its oily residues inward would be biochemically limited?","o":["It couldn’t interact with the cell","It would be more active, as more of its surface is oily","It would become DNA","It would form disulphides","It would be a chaperone"],"e":"Leaving hydrophobic parts “grumpy in the corner” would make a molecule that couldn't interact with the cell – little catalysis.","w":["It would do little or no catalysis.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Exposed oil = no function.","rn":[["","Why not leave a protein fully hydrophobic?","Why not leave a protein fully hydrophobic?","Couldn't interact with cell; Limited catalysis"]]},{"id":26068,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"What drives hydrophobic residues into the protein core?","o":["Water entropy plus van der Waals","Disulphide bonds alone pulling the hydrophobics together","Peptide bonds pushing","Chaperones alone","Charge repulsion"],"e":"(1) Entropic effect of water (far more water than protein); (2) van der Waals – thousands make a strong core.","w":["Entropy of water and van der Waals.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Water entropy + vdW.","rn":[["","What 2 things push the hydrophobic residues together?","What 2 things push the hydrophobic residues together?","Water entropy effect; Van der Waals forces"]]},{"id":26069,"t":"Protein Mutations","s":"What happens to a protein put into olive oil?","o":["The opposite problem","It folds perfectly, as oil supports the hydrophobic core","It becomes DNA","It forms disulphides","Nothing"],"e":"Unless you dump the protein in olive oil: then the opposite problem; oil also denatures.","w":["The driving force reverses.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Oil reverses hydrophobic effect.","rn":[["","Why is valine a problem on the outside?","Why is valine a problem on the outside?","Hydrophobic"],["","Anti-parallel β-sheet","Anti-parallel β-sheet","Adjacent strands run; Opposite directions"]]},{"id":26070,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Which statement about loops in protein structure is correct?","o":["Unstructured stretches that “waft around”","Rigid α-helices linking sheets","Disulphide bonds","Always β-sheets","They don’t exist"],"e":"Loops are similar to turns: unstructured between elements, not really classified – they “just waft around”.","w":["Loops are unstructured.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Loops are flexible.","rn":[["","Loops","Loops","Unstructured stretches between secondary structures; Not really classified"]]},{"id":26071,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Which structure in green fluorescent protein holds the fluorophore?","o":["A β-barrel of anti-parallel strands","A β-barrel of parallel α-helices","A single α-helix","A haem group","A molten globule"],"e":"β-barrel from jellyfish GFP: anti-parallel strands rolled into a barrel with the fluorophore in the centre.","w":["Strands.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GFP β-barrel.","rn":[["","Beta-barrel","Beta-barrel","Anti-parallel β-sheet; Forming a barrel"]]},{"id":26072,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Which representation shows secondary structure without all atoms?","o":["Cartoon format","Space-filling","Primary sequence letters","Chemical formula","Electron micrograph"],"e":"Structural biology uses <b>cartoon format</b> to show secondary structure – showing all atoms is too confusing.","w":["All atoms.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cartoon format.","rn":[["","Cartoon format","Cartoon format","Shows secondary structure; Without every atom"]]},{"id":26073,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"What is the electrostatic surface of a protein?","o":["A map of partial surface charges","A map of disulphide bonds across the surface","Its sequence","Its hydrophobic core","Its signal sequence"],"e":"Electrostatic interactions can be partial charges across the surface; mapping this = <b>electrostatic surface</b>.","w":["Charges.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Electrostatic surface.","rn":[["","What can electrostatic interactions also form across a protein surface?","What can electrostatic interactions also form across a protein surface?","Partial charges"]]},{"id":26074,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Where are protein-protein interfaces in quaternary structure mainly held together?","o":["Non-covalent interactions","Peptide bonds","Interchain disulphides always","DNA","Water only"],"e":"Interfaces have lots of non-covalent interactions; disulphides between chains are rare.","w":["Within chains.","Rare.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Interfaces: non-covalent.","rn":[["","What mainly causes non-covalent interactions?","What mainly causes non-covalent interactions?","Charge"],["","What unfolds proteins?","What unfolds proteins?","Solvents breaking non-covalent interactions; And reducing disulphides"],["","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","What are the 3 non-covalent side chain interactions?","Electrostatic attractions; Hydrogen bonds; Van der Waals attractions"]]},{"id":26075,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Name the amino acid often found in β-turns and at the edges of β-sheets, whose side chain rings back to its own nitrogen.","e":"Proline's ring restricts rotation, favouring turns.","k":"Proline.","a":["proline","pro","p"],"rn":[["","Which amino acid tends to be at the edges of β-sheets?","Which amino acid tends to be at the edges of β-sheets?","Proline"],["","Which amino acid helps cause β-turns?","Which amino acid helps cause β-turns?","Proline"]]},{"id":26076,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"What fraction of amino acids in proteins are in turns or loops?","o":["Nearly one third","About 1%","Nearly all","About 10%","About two thirds"],"e":"Nearly <b>one third</b> of amino acids are in turns or loops.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"~1/3 in turns/loops.","rn":[["","What fraction of amino acids are in turns or loops?","What fraction of amino acids are in turns or loops?","Nearly one third"]]},{"id":26077,"t":"Primary Structure","s":"Which statement about the direction of hydrogen bonding in α-helices is correct?","o":["C=O of residue n to N–H of n+4","C=O of residue n to N–H of n+1","Between separate chains","Between R groups only","There are none"],"e":"α-helix: H-bond via a carboxyl of one amino acid and amide H <b>4 residues later</b>.","w":["n+4.","β-sheet.","Backbone.","Not."],"k":"α-helix n → n+4.","rn":[["","Which groups form the α-helix H bond?","Which groups form the α-helix H bond?","Carboxyl of one residue; Amide H 4 residues later"]]},{"id":26078,"t":"Chaperones","s":"A long protein exits the ribosome into water and begins misfolding. What is most likely to help?","o":["Chaperones","Adding urea","Removing disulphides","Adding a signal sequence","Lowering pH"],"e":"Large complex proteins probably won't fold correctly alone; chaperones guide them.","w":["Denatures.","Not.","Targeting.","Denatures."],"k":"Chaperones for complex proteins.","rn":[["","Chaperones","Chaperones","Proteins that guide; Others to fold correctly"]]},{"id":26079,"t":"Protein Denaturation","s":"Which statement about denaturation by pH changes is correct?","o":["Acid or alkali can denature; some refold","pH never affects proteins, as they buffer themselves","pH always refolds proteins","pH only affects DNA","pH forms peptide bonds"],"e":"Changing pH can denature; often refolds, often doesn't.","w":["pH can denature.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"pH can denature.","rn":[["","Do proteins always refold after acid or alkali (pH change)?","Do proteins always refold after acid or alkali (pH change)?","Often they refold; Often they do not"],["","What 2 other things can denature a protein?","What 2 other things can denature a protein?","Acid or alkali (pH); Olive oil"]]},{"id":26080,"t":"Primary Structure","s":"What is the significance of the phi and psi angles?","o":["Rotation around bonds flanking the peptide bond","Rotation around the peptide bond itself, which is free","Disulphide angles","pH","Hydrogen bond lengths"],"e":"No rotation around the peptide bond, but rotation on the two flanking bonds: <b>phi and psi</b> angles.","w":["The peptide bond is rigid.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Phi/psi = flanking rotation.","rn":[["","What are the 2 rotation angles beside the peptide bond?","What are the 2 rotation angles beside the peptide bond?","PHI; PSI"],["","What does \"rigid and planar\" mean for the peptide bond?","What does \"rigid and planar\" mean for the peptide bond?","No rotation around the bond"]]},{"id":26081,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Which pairing of protein and dominant secondary structure is correct?","o":["Myoglobin – mostly α-helices","Myoglobin – β-barrel","TNF-α – mostly α-helices","Aquaporin – β-sheets","GFP – α-helices"],"e":"Myoglobin α-helical; TNF-α β-sheets; aquaporin α-helical; GFP β-barrel.","w":["Not.","β-sheets.","α-helical.","β-barrel."],"k":"Myoglobin α-helical."},{"id":26082,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"What term describes a protein complex made of four identical chains?","e":"Homotetramer = four identical chains; haemoglobin is a heterotetramer of two α and two β chains.","k":"Homotetramer.","a":["homotetramer","homo-tetramer"],"rn":[["","Homotetramer","Homotetramer","Tetramer = four; Homo = all the same"]]},{"id":26083,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"Technically, “the haemoglobin protein” refers to:","o":["The monomer; the whole is a complex","Always the tetramer of four chains","The haem group","The gene","The erythrocyte"],"e":"Strictly a <b>protein complex</b>; “the haemoglobin protein” technically means the monomer; structural biologists say “the tetramer”.","w":["Technically the monomer.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Monomer vs complex."},{"id":26084,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"How can scientists determine N and C termini in a solved structure without arrows?","o":["From the chemistry of the model","By colour only, as N is always blue","They can’t","By guessing","By size"],"e":"Student Q: by the chemistry – when a structure is solved a model is built into the data.","w":["By the chemistry when a model is built.","They can.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Chemistry identifies termini.","rn":[["","Non-polar aliphatic R groups: chemistry?","Non-polar aliphatic R groups: chemistry?","Hydrophobic (water phobic)"],["","Polar R groups: chemistry?","Polar R groups: chemistry?","Polar uncharged; Hydrophilic (water loving)"],["","Aromatic R groups: chemistry?","Aromatic R groups: chemistry?","Hydrophobic; Functional groups may alter polarity"]]},{"id":26085,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which interaction acts like a rigid covalent bracket within a protein?","o":["Disulphide bond","Hydrogen bond","Van der Waals","Salt bridge","Peptide bond"],"e":"Disulphide: “like a metal bracket”.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Metal bracket = disulphide.","rn":[["","What is a disulphide bond?","What is a disulphide bond?","Covalent bond; Between 2 cysteines; S-S"],["","Intrachain Disulphide Bond","Intrachain Disulphide Bond","Within the same chain"],["","Which amino acid forms disulphide bonds?","Which amino acid forms disulphide bonds?","Cysteine"]]},{"id":26086,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Which statement about protein folding being spontaneous is correct?","o":["Spontaneous and quick, but some need chaperones","Spontaneous and quick, and always perfect","Always needs ATP and chaperones","Never occurs without chaperones","Random"],"e":"Folding tends to be spontaneous and energetically quick; not always perfect – chaperones help.","w":["Not always perfect.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Spontaneous but imperfect.","rn":[["","Is spontaneous folding always perfect?","Is spontaneous folding always perfect?","No; Some need help"]]},{"id":26087,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"Which class of R group is described as hydrophobic but with possible altered polarity due to functional groups?","o":["Aromatic","Non-polar aliphatic","Polar uncharged","Positive","Negative"],"e":"<b>Aromatic</b> (Phe, Trp, Tyr): hydrophobic but functional groups may alter polarity.","w":["Purely hydrophobic.","Hydrophilic.","Charged.","Charged."],"k":"Aromatic class.","rn":[["","Aromatic R groups: chemistry?","Aromatic R groups: chemistry?","Hydrophobic; Functional groups may alter polarity"]]},{"id":26088,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Why is the ordered water shell around a hydrophobic molecule unfavourable?","o":["It lowers water’s entropy","It raises water’s entropy","It raises the temperature","It forms covalent bonds","It adds charge"],"e":"Ordered shell → entropy of water decreases – entropically unfavourable; water would rather be random.","w":["Lowers.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ordered water = unfavourable.","rn":[["","Image card: What is water forming around this hydrophobic molecule? What happens to entropy?","What is water forming around this hydrophobic molecule? What happens to entropy?","Ordered solvent shell; Entropy decreases (unfavourable)"],["","Ordered water arrangement: effect on entropy?","Ordered water arrangement: effect on entropy?","Decrease in entropy; Unfavourable"]]},{"id":26089,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Which statement is true of proline's structure?","o":["Its side chain rings back to its own N","Its side chain is just a hydrogen, so it bends","Side chain contains sulphur","Negatively charged","Aromatic"],"e":"Proline: side chain loops back to its own nitrogen to make a ring – induces turns.","w":["That is glycine.","Cysteine.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Proline ring → turns.","rn":[["","How can one chain form an anti-parallel sheet?","How can one chain form an anti-parallel sheet?","Turns and comes back"]]},{"id":26090,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"Name the amino acid whose side chain is just a hydrogen.","e":"Glycine has H as its R group.","k":"Glycine.","a":["glycine","gly","g"],"rn":[["","Gly (G)","Gly (G)","Glycine"],["","What are the 4 special case amino acids?","What are the 4 special case amino acids?","Cysteine; Selenocysteine; Glycine; Proline"]]},{"id":26091,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Which statement about native protein stability is correct?","o":["Many weak interactions, mainly hydrophobic","One covalent bond holds the fold","Only peptide bonds matter","Disulphides dominate all proteins","Folding is independent of interactions"],"e":"Numerous weak interactions; <b>hydrophobic interactions predominate</b> (in red on the slide).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Weak interactions, hydrophobic dominate.","rn":[["","What maintains the folded conformation?","What maintains the folded conformation?","Numerous weak interactions"],["","How strong is one van der Waals interaction?","How strong is one van der Waals interaction?","Rubbish; Very weak"]]},{"id":26092,"t":"Chaperones","s":"What is the relationship between protein shape and catalytic function, per chaperone discussion?","o":["Wrong shape may mean no catalytic site","Shape doesn’t matter if the sequence is correct","Misfolded proteins work better","Function depends only on sequence letters","Catalysis depends on DNA"],"e":"Without chaperones some proteins don't get the right shape, so may not have the right catalytic site.","w":["Shape matters.","Not.","Shape.","Not."],"k":"Shape → function.","rn":[["","What happens without chaperones?","What happens without chaperones?","Wrong 3D shape; May lack catalytic site; Does not work"]]},{"id":26093,"t":"Signal Sequences","s":"Which organelle sorts proteins for their destinations, like a post office?","o":["The Golgi","The nucleus","The ribosome","Mitochondria","Lysosome"],"e":"Protein made in the ER then via the <b>Golgi</b>, a post office; proteins contain postcodes (signal sequences).","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Golgi = post office.","rn":[["","What analogy is used for the Golgi?","What analogy is used for the Golgi?","A post office"]]},{"id":26094,"t":"Protein Mutations","s":"A cytosolic enzyme’s single surface glutamate is replaced by valine and the molecules begin to stick together. Which disease works this way?","o":["Sickle cell anaemia","Cystic fibrosis","Down syndrome","Xeroderma pigmentosum","Charcot–Marie–Tooth 1A"],"e":"Sickle cell anaemia: GLU → VAL in haemoglobin. One change puts a hydrophobic valine outside the molecule, so haemoglobins stick together in chains that distort red cells into a sickle shape.","w":["CF was the splicing example in gene expression.","Trisomy.","Repair defect.","Duplication."],"k":"Foundations, not biochemistry.","rn":[["","Sickle Cell Anaemia","Sickle Cell Anaemia","Inherited disorder affecting haemoglobin; GLU to VAL"]]},{"id":26095,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which amino acid pair forms a classic salt bridge?","o":["Lysine and glutamic acid","Cysteine and cysteine","Valine and alanine","Glycine and proline","Tyrosine and phenylalanine"],"e":"Lys (+) and Glu (−) – electrostatic attraction.","w":["Disulphide.","Hydrophobic.","Special.","Aromatic."],"k":"Lys–Glu salt bridge.","rn":[["","Which 2 amino acids show an electrostatic attraction?","Which 2 amino acids show an electrostatic attraction?","Glutamic acid; Lysine"],["","Glu (E)","Glu (E)","Glutamic acid"]]},{"id":26096,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Name the interaction between the uncharged methyl groups of valine and alanine.","e":"Van der Waals interactions arise from temporary dipoles.","k":"Van der Waals.","a":["van der waals","van der waals interaction","van der waals forces","vdw","london forces","dispersion forces"],"rn":[["","Weakest 2 protein interactions?","Weakest 2 protein interactions?","3. Hydrogen bond; 4. Van der Waals"],["","What groups interact by van der Waals forces?","What groups interact by van der Waals forces?","Methyl groups (CH3)"],["","How strong is one van der Waals interaction?","How strong is one van der Waals interaction?","Rubbish; Very weak"]]},{"id":26097,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Which statement about hydrogen bonds in DNA and proteins is correct?","o":["Both use H-bonds (DNA 2–3 per pair)","Only DNA uses H-bonds; proteins use covalent bonds","Only proteins use H-bonds","H-bonds are covalent in DNA","Neither"],"e":"As in DNA (three in one base pair, two in the other); proteins use them too.","w":["Proteins use many too.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"H-bonds in DNA and proteins.","rn":[["","What group do cysteines use to form disulphide bonds?","What group do cysteines use to form disulphide bonds?","SH (sulphur + hydrogen)"]]},{"id":26098,"t":"Protein Domains","s":"Which best describes a sequence motif?","o":["A recognisable stretch marking a domain","A 3-D structure formed by several chains","A disulphide","A chaperone","A signal peptide only"],"e":"Domains can be “found” as <b>SEQUENCE MOTIFS</b> in the primary sequence.","w":["A motif is in the primary sequence.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sequence motif.","rn":[["","Sequence motif","Sequence motif","Similar amino acid stretch; Shared by same-job domains"]]},{"id":26099,"t":"Chaperones","s":"Which explains why the folding problem would be impossible without restrictions?","o":["Full rotation gives ~10⁴⁰ shapes","Proteins have no bonds that could restrict rotation","Proteins are too small to fold","Water prevents folding","Amino acids are identical"],"e":"Full rotation → ~10⁴⁰; peptide bond rigidity reduces to ~10¹².","w":["Peptide bond rigidity restricts it.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"10⁴⁰ without rigidity.","rn":[["","Possible fold combinations with full rotation?","Possible fold combinations with full rotation?","About 10^40"]]},{"id":26101,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"A protein's cysteines are mutated to serines. What is the likely structural consequence?","o":["Loss of disulphides, so more misfolding","More disulphides, so less misfolding","Faster peptide bond formation","No change ever","It becomes DNA"],"e":"Disulphides reduce misfolding; removing cysteines removes them.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Lose Cys → lose S–S."},{"id":26102,"t":"Protein Domains","s":"Which of these is <b>not</b> a role of protein domains listed?","o":["Storing DNA","DNA binding","Protease activity","Spanning plasma membranes","(All of the others are listed)"],"e":"Domain roles: DNA binding, protease, spanning membranes.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Option 1 not listed."],"k":"Domain roles.","rn":[["","What are 3 example roles of domains?","What are 3 example roles of domains?","DNA binding; Protease; Spanning plasma membranes"]]},{"id":26103,"t":"Levels of Protein Structure","s":"Which structural level does the sickle cell mutation directly change?","o":["Primary structure","Only quaternary structure, via the haem groups","Only secondary structure","DNA packaging","No level"],"e":"One amino acid change (primary) → surface valine → abnormal intermolecular association.","w":["The change is in one amino acid.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Sickle: primary change.","rn":[["","What are the 4 levels of protein structure?","What are the 4 levels of protein structure?","Primary; Secondary; Tertiary; Quaternary; MNEMONIC"],["","Primary structure in one phrase?","Primary structure in one phrase?","Sequence of amino acids"],["","What determines secondary structure?","What determines secondary structure?","Primary structure"]]},{"id":26104,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Which statement about protein folding being “not random, not magic” is correct?","o":["It follows the laws of physics","It is random","It is controlled by DNA directly","It requires magic","It depends on light"],"e":"“It's not random, it's not magic. It's based upon the laws of physics.”","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Folding obeys physics."},{"id":26107,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Two strands of a β-sheet run in the same N→C direction. How do their hydrogen bonds look?","o":["Slightly skewed – a parallel sheet","Straight across – a parallel sheet","Straight across – an anti-parallel sheet","Absent, as R groups pair instead","Between R groups above and below"],"e":"Parallel β-sheet: all strands run in the same direction and the H-bonds are slightly skewed (slanted). Anti-parallel: neighbouring strands run in opposite directions (down, up, down) and the H-bonds go straight across.","w":["Parallel bonds are skewed.","Anti-parallel strands run opposite.","Backbone H-bonds exist.","R groups protrude; they don’t bond."],"k":"Alberts ch 3.","rn":[["","How do hydrogen bonds look in a parallel β-sheet?","How do hydrogen bonds look in a parallel β-sheet?","Slightly skewed (squint)"]]},{"id":26108,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Cysteine residues in a protein are about 4 Å apart in a reducing environment. What is their state?","o":["Free SH groups – no disulphide","Linked by a disulphide bond","Linked by a peptide bond","Linked by a salt bridge","Converted to methionine"],"e":"Disulphide bonds form when two cysteine SH groups within about 4 Å are oxidised (hydrogens removed, sulphurs bond covalently). Reduction breaks the bond, so in a reducing environment the cysteines stay as free SH groups.","w":["Disulphides need oxidation.","Not.","Not charged.","Not."],"k":"PDB = structures.","rn":[["","What group do cysteines use to form disulphide bonds?","What group do cysteines use to form disulphide bonds?","SH (sulphur + hydrogen)"],["","Why is cysteine special?","Why is cysteine special?","Sulphur in R group; Forms disulphide bonds"]]},{"id":26109,"t":"Signal Sequences","s":"A protein lacks any targeting sequence but is found in the nucleus. What is the most consistent explanation?","o":["It would be expected in the cytosol","All proteins go to the nucleus by default","Signal sequences are irrelevant","It must be a chaperone","It must be DNA"],"e":"Without a signal sequence proteins stay cytosolic by default; nuclear proteins have a nuclear postcode.","w":["Default is cytosol.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nuclear proteins need signal.","rn":[["","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","The cytosol"]]},{"id":26110,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Which statement about β-sheet H-bonding in parallel sheets is correct?","o":["Slightly skewed","Straight across","Absent","Covalent","Between R groups"],"e":"Parallel sheets: H-bonds “ever so slightly skewed… squint”.","w":["Anti-parallel.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Parallel H-bonds skewed.","rn":[["","How do hydrogen bonds look in a parallel β-sheet?","How do hydrogen bonds look in a parallel β-sheet?","Slightly skewed (squint)"]]},{"id":26111,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Which explains why disulphide bonds rarely link separate chains in haemoglobin?","o":["Interfaces are mainly non-covalent","Haemoglobin has no cysteines at all","The chains are covalently fused","Haem forms the disulphides","DNA links the chains"],"e":"Interfaces: non-covalent; interchain disulphides rare (lecture).","w":["Not stated.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Interfaces non-covalent.","rn":[["","What mainly causes non-covalent interactions?","What mainly causes non-covalent interactions?","Charge"]]},{"id":26112,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"Which statement summarises the molten globule state?","o":["A partly folded ball with hydrophobics mostly inside","A partly folded ball with hydrophobics mostly outside","The fully native structure","The unfolded chain","A disulphide-linked dimer"],"e":"Molten globule: rough ball with red (hydrophobic) dots mostly inside.","w":["Inside.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Molten globule.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of folding via the molten globule?","What are the 3 steps of folding via the molten globule?","Unfolded protein; Hydrophobic collapse; Internal core rearrangement"]]},{"id":26113,"t":"Signal Sequences","s":"Which pairing about signal sequence destinations is correct?","o":["ER, nucleus or an organelle","Only the nucleus","Only the plasma membrane","Only lysosomes","Only outside the cell"],"e":"Signal sequences can send proteins to the ER, nucleus or an organelle.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Signal destinations.","rn":[["","What are 3 signal sequence destinations?","What are 3 signal sequence destinations?","ER; Nuclear; Organelle"]]},{"id":26114,"t":"Levels of Protein Structure","s":"How long is a typical signal sequence?","o":["About 15 amino acids","About 1,000 amino acids","One amino acid","146 bp","3 nucleotides"],"e":"Signal sequence: short stretch of residues (<b>15 aa</b>).","w":["Not.","Not.","Nucleosome.","Codon."],"k":"~15 aa signal.","rn":[["","Primary structure in one phrase?","Primary structure in one phrase?","Sequence of amino acids"],["","Sequence motif","Sequence motif","Similar amino acid stretch; Shared by same-job domains"],["","What can a DNA mutation change?","What can a DNA mutation change?","The amino acid sequence"]]},{"id":26116,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"What is the effect of a disulphide bond on the number of misfolding pathways?","o":["It reduces them","It increases them","No effect","It doubles them","It causes denaturation"],"e":"Disulphide bonds <b>reduce</b> the number of ways a protein can MISFOLD.","w":["Not.","Effect.","Not.","Not."],"k":"S–S reduces misfolding.","rn":[["","How can a disulphide bond be broken?","How can a disulphide bond be broken?","Reducing reagents; Significant heating"],["","Why is sickling a problem?","Why is sickling a problem?","Reduces oxygen carriage"],["","What unfolds proteins?","What unfolds proteins?","Solvents breaking non-covalent interactions; And reducing disulphides"]]},{"id":26117,"t":"Primary Structure","s":"What is the effect of the peptide bond on folding possibilities?","o":["It reduces the ways a protein can fold","It increases the ways a protein can fold","It has no effect","It causes misfolding","It forms disulphides"],"e":"The peptide bond <b>reduces the number of possible ways a protein can fold</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Peptide bond limits folds.","rn":[["","What does the rigid peptide bond reduce?","What does the rigid peptide bond reduce?","Ways a protein can fold; Motion between amino acids"]]},{"id":26118,"t":"Protein Mutations","s":"A Glu → Val substitution puts valine on haemoglobin's surface, so molecules stick together in chains. Name the disease.","e":"Sickle cell anaemia changes primary structure; the trait reduces the chance of getting malaria.","k":"Sickle cell anaemia.","a":["sickle cell anaemia","sickle cell disease","sickle cell anemia","sickle cell","scd"],"rn":[["","Sickle Cell Anaemia","Sickle Cell Anaemia","Inherited disorder affecting haemoglobin; GLU to VAL"]]},{"id":26119,"t":"Bonds Maintaining Structure","s":"Why is a “salt bridge” really just an electrostatic interaction?","o":["Opposite charges attract; no shared electrons","Opposite charges share electrons, forming a covalent link","It involves salt crystals","It is a hydrogen bond","It requires sulphur"],"e":"Textbooks call it a salt bridge, but it's just electrostatic attraction.","w":["Electrostatic, not covalent.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Salt bridge = electrostatic.","rn":[["","How can uncharged hydrophobic groups attract each other?","How can uncharged hydrophobic groups attract each other?","Electron clouds unevenly spread; Slight + and - charges"],["","What is Electrostatic Interaction?","What is Electrostatic Interaction?","Attraction between charges; Positive attracts negative"]]},{"id":26121,"t":"Protein Mutations","s":"A 10-year-old child has repeated painful crises and anaemia. Blood films show distorted red blood cells. Genetic testing finds a single DNA change that replaces glutamic acid with valine at one position on the surface of the haemoglobin protein.<br><br>Why does such a small change cause disease?","o":["A charged residue becomes hydrophobic, altering a critical surface area so chains interact and distort red cells","It deletes the whole haemoglobin gene","It adds a signal sequence that sends haemoglobin to the nucleus","It breaks every peptide bond in the chain","It replaces a hydrophobic residue with a charged one, so the protein becomes more soluble in water"],"e":"Step 1: glutamic acid (Glu) is a negatively charged, hydrophilic amino acid, while valine (Val) is hydrophobic, so the R group chemistry on the protein surface changes. Step 2: mutations that change the amino acid sequence can alter structure or a small but critical area affecting function and interactions; in sickle cell anaemia the mutant Hb chains interact and distort erythrocytes.","w":["It is a single amino acid change, not a gene deletion.","Signal sequences are short postcodes; none is added here.","Only one residue changes; peptide bonds remain.","Glu (charged) is replaced by Val (hydrophobic), not the reverse."],"k":"Sickle cell: Glu → Val (charged → hydrophobic) on Hb surface.","rn":[["","What 2 things can an amino acid change do?","What 2 things can an amino acid change do?","Alter tertiary structure; Change a small critical area"]]},{"id":26122,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"A researcher studies a soluble enzyme. In its folded form, most of its non-polar amino acids such as leucine and isoleucine are buried in the centre, while charged and polar residues face the water. When she mutates several surface residues to hydrophobic ones, the protein folds poorly and clumps together.<br><br>Which principle drives normal folding, and why?","o":["Hydrophobic interactions: burying non-polar groups frees ordered water, increasing entropy","Disulphide bonds alone determine folding","Peptide bonds rotate freely, so any fold is possible","Folding increases free energy, so proteins are very stable","Hydrogen bonds with water around the non-polar residues are what hold the folded protein together"],"e":"Step 1: water around a hydrophobic molecule forms an ordered solvent shell, an unfavourable decrease in entropy, whereas clustering non-polar groups presents the smallest area to water, a favourable increase in entropy. Step 2: this hydrophobic effect is the major driving force to bury non-polar amino acids, and hydrophobic interactions predominate among the weak interactions stabilising folded proteins.","w":["Disulphide bonds help, but hydrophobic interactions predominate.","The peptide bond is rigid and planar, limiting folds.","Proteins fold to the lowest free energy and are only marginally stable.","Ordered water around non-polar groups is unfavourable; burying them drives folding."],"k":"Hydrophobic effect (entropy gain) drives folding.","rn":[["","What is the major driving force to bury non-polar amino acids?","What is the major driving force to bury non-polar amino acids?","Increase in water entropy"]]},{"id":26123,"t":"Tertiary and Quaternary Structure","s":"A scientist adds urea and a reducing agent to a solution of ribonuclease. The enzyme loses its shape and activity. When she slowly removes both substances, the enzyme regains its shape and full activity without any other help.<br><br>What happened, and what does the recovery show?","o":["Denaturation; the primary sequence determines the final tertiary structure","Hydrolysis of peptide bonds; the enzyme was remade from new amino acids","Mutation; the gene was repaired","Denaturation; chaperones such as GroEL-ES must always be present to refold proteins","Quaternary assembly; extra chains were added"],"e":"Step 1: solvents that break non-covalent interactions (and reducing agents that break disulphides) unfold proteins, which is denaturation, and some proteins such as ribonuclease can recover when the substance is removed. Step 2: refolding unaided shows that the primary sequence determines the final tertiary structure.","w":["Denaturants break non-covalent interactions, not peptide bonds.","No gene change occurred.","Ribonuclease refolded without chaperones.","No chains were added."],"k":"Ribonuclease refolds → primary sequence determines tertiary structure.","rn":[["","What determines the final tertiary structure?","What determines the final tertiary structure?","The primary sequence"]]},{"id":26124,"t":"Levels of Protein Structure","s":"A biochemist describes haemoglobin in four ways. Description 1 lists the amino acids from the N- to C-terminus. Description 2 notes local α-helices. Description 3 gives the overall 3-D shape of one globin chain. Description 4 describes how four globin chains fit together.<br><br>Which levels of structure are these?","o":["1 primary; 2 secondary; 3 tertiary; 4 quaternary","1 secondary; 2 primary; 3 quaternary; 4 tertiary","1 primary; 2 tertiary; 3 secondary; 4 quaternary","1 tertiary; 2 secondary; 3 primary; 4 quaternary","1 primary; 2 secondary; 3 quaternary; 4 tertiary, as one chain's 3-D shape is the highest level"],"e":"Step 1: primary structure is the linear sequence of amino acids (N → C), and secondary structure is local folding such as α-helices, β-sheets and turns. Step 2: tertiary structure is the 3-D shape of one folded chain, and quaternary structure is the arrangement of multiple chains, as in haemoglobin.","w":["The sequence is primary, not secondary.","Local helices are secondary, not tertiary.","The sequence is primary, not tertiary.","The arrangement of several chains is quaternary."],"k":"Primary: sequence; secondary: local; tertiary: one chain 3-D; quaternary: several chains.","rn":[["","What are the 4 levels of protein structure?","What are the 4 levels of protein structure?","Primary; Secondary; Tertiary; Quaternary; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":26125,"t":"Signal Sequences","s":"A protein that normally works in the endoplasmic reticulum is found floating in the cytosol of a patient's cells. Sequencing shows that a short stretch of about 15 amino acids at one end of the protein has been deleted, while the rest of the protein is normal.<br><br>What has been lost, and why does the protein stay in the cytosol?","o":["A signal sequence acting as a postcode; proteins without one stay in the cytosol","A disulphide bond; without it the protein cannot leave the cytosol","A protein domain for DNA binding","The quaternary structure, so the protein cannot move","An α-helix; without helices proteins cannot be transported to any organelle in the cell"],"e":"Step 1: a signal sequence is a short stretch of residues (about 15 aa) that acts as a postcode, sending proteins to the ER, the nucleus or an organelle. Step 2: proteins lacking a signal sequence remain in the cytosol.","w":["Disulphide bonds stabilise structure; they are not postcodes.","A DNA-binding domain is not a targeting signal.","Loss of 15 residues at one end is a signal sequence, not quaternary structure.","Targeting depends on a signal sequence, not helices."],"k":"Signal sequence (~15 aa) = postcode; without one → cytosol.","rn":[["","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","Where do proteins lacking a signal sequence stay?","The cytosol"]]},{"id":26126,"t":"Thermodynamics of Protein Folding","s":"A researcher studies an extracellular protein that remains stable in a harsh environment. Treating it with a reducing agent breaks bonds between cysteine residues and makes it much less stable. Separately, a lysine and a glutamate buried in the protein's hydrophobic interior form an interaction that strongly stabilises the fold.<br><br>Which bonds are these?","o":["Disulphide bonds (covalent) and a salt bridge (electrostatic, stronger in the interior)","Peptide bonds and van der Waals forces","Hydrogen bonds and disulphide bonds","Van der Waals forces and peptide bonds","Salt bridges between the cysteines, and a disulphide bond between lysine and glutamate"],"e":"Step 1: disulphide bonds are covalent bonds formed between two cysteine sulphur atoms by oxidation and are broken by reducing agents; they join different parts of the chain. Step 2: an electrostatic interaction (salt bridge) forms between opposite charges such as Lys (+) and Glu (−), and its strength increases when it forms in the more hydrophobic interior.","w":["Peptide bonds link the backbone, and the Lys–Glu interaction is electrostatic.","Cys–Cys bonds are disulphides, and Lys–Glu is a salt bridge.","Van der Waals forces are between uncharged groups.","The pairs are swapped."],"k":"Cys–Cys = disulphide (covalent); Lys–Glu = salt bridge (stronger buried).","rn":[["","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","When does a salt bridge get stronger?","Inside the hydrophobic interior"]]},{"id":26127,"t":"Secondary Structure","s":"Two segments of a protein are analysed. Segment A forms a right-handed spiral in which the C=O of each residue hydrogen-bonds to the N–H four residues later. Segment B forms extended zigzag strands lying side by side, with hydrogen bonds between neighbouring strands and small R groups above and below.<br><br>Which secondary structures are these?","o":["A: α-helix; B: β-sheet","A: β-sheet; B: α-helix","A: β-turn; B: α-helix","A: α-helix; B: β-turn","A: quaternary structure; B: tertiary structure"],"e":"Step 1: the α-helix is a right-handed helix stabilised by hydrogen bonds from the carboxyl of one amino acid to an amide hydrogen four residues later (3.6 residues per turn). Step 2: the β-sheet is an extended zigzag conformation with hydrogen bonds between adjacent strands, R groups above and below, and needs relatively small R groups.","w":["The two are swapped.","A β-turn is a 180° change in direction.","Segment B is an extended sheet, not a turn.","These are local, secondary structures."],"k":"α-helix: C=O to N–H +4; β-sheet: zigzag, H-bonds between strands.","rn":[["","Which groups form the α-helix H bond?","Which groups form the α-helix H bond?","Carboxyl of one residue; Amide H 4 residues later"],["","β-sheet","β-sheet","Extended zigzag; Of the polypeptide chain"],["","What are the 3 types of secondary structure?","What are the 3 types of secondary structure?","β-sheet (parallel and anti-parallel); α-helix; β-turns"]]},{"id":26128,"t":"Primary Structure","s":"A student builds a model of a polypeptide chain and finds that some bonds in the backbone cannot rotate, so the atoms around them stay in a flat plane. This greatly limits the shapes the chain can take as it folds.<br><br>Which bond is this, and why is it rigid?","o":["The peptide bond; it has partial double bond character","The disulphide bond; it is a full double bond","Hydrogen bonds; they are covalent","Van der Waals bonds; they are the strongest","The peptide bond; it is a single bond, so it rotates freely in every direction"],"e":"Step 1: amino acids are linked by peptide bonds, which are neither single nor double bonds, with a length in between. Step 2: the peptide bond has partial double bond character, making it rigid and planar, which limits the possible folds of the chain.","w":["Disulphides link cysteines, not the backbone, and are not double bonds.","Hydrogen bonds are non-covalent.","Van der Waals forces are the weakest individually.","The peptide bond is rigid, not freely rotating."],"k":"Peptide bond: partial double bond → rigid, planar.","rn":[["","What character does the peptide bond have?","What character does the peptide bond have?","Partial double bond character"]]},{"id":26129,"t":"Chaperones","s":"A large protein has three regions with separate jobs: one binds DNA, one cuts other proteins, and one spans the plasma membrane. When made in cells lacking a helper complex called GroEL-ES, the protein often misfolds and does not work.<br><br>What are the three regions, and what does GroEL-ES do?","o":["Domains with distinct functions; GroEL-ES is a chaperone that helps folding","Signal sequences; GroEL-ES cuts the protein into pieces","Secondary structures; GroEL-ES adds disulphide bonds","Quaternary subunits; GroEL-ES is a denaturant","Primary sequences; GroEL-ES is a protease domain that breaks peptide bonds in misfolded proteins"],"e":"Step 1: proteins may fold into multiple domains, each usually with a distinctive function such as DNA binding, protease activity or spanning membranes, often seen as sequence motifs. Step 2: some proteins need help to fold correctly, and chaperones such as the GroEL-ES complex guide them to fold.","w":["Signal sequences are postcodes, and GroEL-ES helps folding.","Domains are functional regions, and GroEL-ES is a chaperone.","The regions are domains of one chain, and GroEL-ES aids folding.","GroEL-ES is a chaperone, not a protease."],"k":"Domains = functional regions; chaperones (GroEL-ES) aid folding.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this complex? What does it do?","What is this complex? What does it do?","GroEL-ES chaperone; Helps proteins fold correctly"]]},{"id":26130,"t":"Amino Acids","s":"A drug designer studies the binding site of a protein. The site contains arginine and lysine residues, which carry a significant charge at pH 7. She wants to design a drug that binds there through electrostatic attraction.<br><br>Which charge should the drug carry, and which class do arginine and lysine belong to?","o":["Negative; positively charged (basic) amino acids","Positive; negatively charged (acidic) amino acids","Uncharged; non-polar aliphatic amino acids","Negative; aromatic hydrophobic amino acids","Positive; polar uncharged amino acids like serine and threonine, which repel positive charges"],"e":"Step 1: R groups give amino acids their chemistry: arginine (R), histidine (H) and lysine (K) are positively charged (basic) and hydrophilic, carrying a significant charge at pH 7. Step 2: electrostatic interactions form between opposite charges, so a negatively charged drug would be attracted to the positive binding site.","w":["Arg and Lys are basic and positive, so a positive drug would be repelled.","Arg and Lys are charged, not non-polar.","Arg and Lys are not aromatic.","Arg and Lys are positively charged, not polar uncharged."],"k":"Arg, His, Lys: positive (basic) at pH 7 → attract negative groups.","rn":[["","What are the 3 positively charged amino acids?","What are the 3 positively charged amino acids?","ARG (R); HIS (H); LYS (K)"],["","Which 2 R group classes are charged?","Which 2 R group classes are charged?","Positive (basic); Negative (acidic)"]]}]);
